From affeed77e870986aa01da237512497c48ac859c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 10:44:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(bedrock): render Character Creator geometry and face
 animations

Character Creator skins can split the body and animated face into separate geometry models and textures. Parse the bound models together, validate their layout, and render the face as a synchronized layer. Keep a visible vanilla fallback for unsupported geometry and animation data instead of installing a blank renderer.

A native Bedrock 1.26.51, protocol 2193 login with the owned Helli Arm added a type-2 image (32 by 512, 16 frames) and animated_32x32 resource-patch binding alongside a type-1 face (32 by 64, two blinking frames). Both geometry descriptions used the full strip height. Normalize their texture size to one frame, remap bottom-origin mesh UVs, and render each bound surface on its own animated texture. Support the corresponding animated_128x128 binding with the same validated strip layout. Body coverage includes surfaces nested below the player bones.

The captured persona mesh UVs use a bottom origin, while Java vertex UVs use a top origin. Flip V when creating mesh vertices so both native and converted GLTF skins keep their texture orientation. Keep raw login data and textures outside the repository. An opt-in test reads a private capture through STACKANVIL_PERSONA_CAPTURE and verifies that every bound animation surface is accepted. Native multiplayer timing still needs a video comparison.

Render menu previews with their actual model and animated layers. The skin widget uses the registered body geometry, and the GUI skin renderer submits additional surfaces with the same pose and independent animation textures. Preview close and skin replacement release those resources through the shared lifecycle.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1's licensed persona.render_controllers.json selects looping
face and body strips with floor(query.life_time * 7), modulo their frame
count. Use seven frames per second and actor render-state age for world
surfaces. Preview lifetime starts when its appearance is created and uses
the monotonic clock. Renderer lookup no longer advances animations with
absolute wall-clock time.

The matching persona blink controller starts with open eyes, evaluates one
state per frame, samples its 3-40 second threshold on every open-state
evaluation, and holds blinking for the sampled 0-0.2 second return time.
Retain transition-frame variable values and do not resample a repeated
render pass at the same lifetime. Reset state when an entity's lifetime
rewinds. Only face surfaces use the blink expression; body strips retain
their linear phase. Targeted tests cover frame boundaries, cyclic phase,
state transitions, repeated evaluation and lifetime reset. These checks
establish controller behavior, not a native multiplayer video comparison.

Reference:
https://github.com/Mojang/bedrock-samples/blob/main/resource_pack/render_controllers/persona.render_controllers.json
https://github.com/Mojang/bedrock-samples/blob/main/resource_pack/animation_controllers/persona.animation_controllers.json

Render the verified animated face, 32x32, and 128x128 persona surfaces
through the shared native lit alpha-test material when visible and not
spectating. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 persona controllers select player_animated,
which inherits entity_alphatest with UV animation. Existing CPU frame
cropping preserves its selected texels with identity shader UVs. Keep
spectator and invisible surfaces on their existing fallback route.

Submit animated skin arm and sleeve surfaces during Java's first-person
hand pass. Matching licensed Bedrock 1.26.51.1 persona render controllers
bind the face, 32x32, and 128x128 surfaces in first person, exposing only
arms and sleeves. Keep each surface's bind pivot and existing frame
texture, and share the native alpha-test material for body and animated layers. Java's hand renderer
does not invoke world render layers, so those surfaces previously vanished.

This completes surface submission where Java draws a hand. Native
first-person graph, camera and item transforms, walking bob, and charged
left-hand visibility require further integration and comparison.

Compile authored poly-mesh polygons with the shared native cube subclass.
Sodium 0.9.2 otherwise draws constructor-time cuboid data, which is a
zero-size placeholder for these meshes. The compiler preserves current
positions, UV corners and normals through reflected model transforms.
